+# Original ATM9 isolated runtime QA
+
+This console harness uses ordinary independent files below a new `artifacts`
+directory. It never copies personal worlds, links into a playing instance, edits
+the source pack, disables the RAM advisory, or changes author options/configs.
+Full source SHA256 records, copied-cache hashes, before/after game snapshots,
+console output, loaded Forge mod IDs and native lifecycle observations stay in
+the fixture. Raw evidence contains local paths and must not be published.
+Java receives its original CmlLib argument tokens through a local argument file
+so a deeply nested independent fixture does not exceed Windows' process command
+limit. That private argument file is evidence, never a publication payload.
+
+`baseline` launches the copied author content directly, without
+`PackInstanceService`, e4steam or the production identity adapter. `integrated`
+uses the real `PackInstanceService`, `ManagedComponentService` and
+`PortableIdentityAdapterService`, in production order. Their normal instance
+preparation is deliberately exercised and captured by the snapshot. This is a
+technical offline QA identity; it does not test the launcher's Steam account
+identity or claim an actual Steam guest/overlay connection.
+
+After those services, integrated mode invokes the production
+`LocalAiProvisioningService.PrepareAsync` and applies its environment bindings
+before starting Java. A pack without `launcher/local-ai.json` receives the same
+ordinary preparation as before. A descriptor prepares the pinned official
+runtime/model and instance configuration in this independent fixture; Verity
+inside Java owns backend start and shutdown. The harness starts no backend and
+does not keep it alive. `local-ai-preparation.json` records file provisioning
+separately from readiness, Russian conversation and game/backend lifetime;
+those runtime checks require their own evidence. No microphone is enabled by
+the harness. File preparation may perform the same large first-run downloads
+as the production Play action.
+
+The Java agent is launch-only test instrumentation, never a Forge mod or a
+distribution file. It performs no bytecode transformation. Agent protocol 3
+supports a separate explicit `observe` permission; this is not a readiness
+claim and needs no visual coordinator. Until `observe` or the legacy `ready`
+marker exists, it does not enumerate Minecraft classes or reflect into game
+code. It then looks only through `Instrumentation.getAllLoadedClasses` and
+checks the existing Minecraft class with JDK17
+`sun.misc.Unsafe.shouldBeInitialized`. It does not load or initialize Minecraft
+to obtain an observation. A class whose initialization is pending, in progress
+or failed is never invoked. Only after successful full initialization does a
+single-signature method handle obtain the existing client instance and queue
+inspection on its native executor.
+
+Inspection records loading/error screens without treating them as healthy. It
+requires three consecutive actual TitleScreen (including PackMenu subclasses)
+samples with no loading overlay and no active world before declaring native
+title health. For QuickPlay, three samples with player, level, integrated server,
+no overlay and no screen establish the equivalent native joined-world
+observation. Only then is the actual Forge ModList recorded. Fresh-world
+creation retains its no-existing-world/no-active-world guards and requires the
+native title condition. None of these observations claims visual quality,
+Steam connectivity or complete mod functionality. Native reads, optional
+creation and normal `Minecraft.stop` use the client executor and game classloader
+context. Fixed symbols are for Minecraft 1.20.1 Forge, with exact prepared
+profile/JAR hashes retained in the evidence.
+
+Agent protocol 4 additionally records the real GPU limit only after that native
+health condition, on the client/render executor. It reads the current GLFW
+context and only `GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE`, vendor, renderer and version. It changes
+no GL state, framebuffer binding, effect or quality setting. An absent context
+or failed probe is explicitly unverified. It does not establish synchronous
+debug output during earlier GL errors.
+
+A fresh v4 launch can also pass one optional
+`--resource-probe namespace:path`. After healthy title and again after healthy
+integrated-world samples it reads the actual selected ResourceManager winner,
+source pack ID, length and SHA256. It never changes a resource or prints its
+bytes/filesystem path. Resource IDs are validated, streams are closed and a
+resource larger than 1 MiB is rejected. Missing resources and probe failures are
+recorded without claiming the expected hash. The exact ResourceManager SRG
+signatures are pinned to Minecraft 1.20.1; older agents reject this CLI option.
+
