+The observation deadline defaults to 45 minutes from agent startup. A fresh v3
+launch can set `--qa-timeout-minutes <1..120>`; both launcher and agent enforce
+the bound. Expiry records a timeout, grants no PASS and never kills the game.
+An already started native creation request is not forcibly aborted on expiry;
+a queued inspection checks the deadline before it starts. A legacy v2 launch
+retains its fixed 45-minute deadline.
+
+Control commands, the live-fixture guard and audits use the same process identity
+check. A matching `exit.json` is terminal without looking up its historical PID;
+Windows may reuse that PID or retain its terminated process object. An exit
+record with a conflicting PID/time or malformed JSON is an actionable evidence
+error, never an active-process assumption. Without terminal evidence the process
+must be Java/javaw and match its recorded OS creation time. New launches retain
+the actual creation time (1 ms tolerance); old records stamped just before
+CreateProcess use a 2-second tolerance. A name/start mismatch never receives a
+control marker. Metadata access failures require retry/review rather than
+claiming that a process has ended. The read-only standalone self-test uses its
